To facilitate accurate marking, your submitted solution must use this template.

To complete each part of the exercise, add your own code to the following files:
ADXL345.c: the Part 1 solution functions are provided (used by all parts). You can add your own functions here.
part1: nothing to do, this solution is provided (part1.c)
part2: nothing to do, this solution is provided (part2.c, accel_kernel_module.c)
part3: part3.c, accel_kernel_module.c
part4: part4.c, accel_kernel_module.c
additional part (optional): your own .c file(s) in the partAdditional folder


Please test your solution using the provided runall.sh script:
part1: ./runall.sh 1
part2: ./runall.sh 2
part3: ./runall.sh 3
part4: ./runall.sh 4
additional part: ./runall.sh additional


ADDITIONAL PART (optional, 1 mark)
Take your part 3 or part 4 and add something new to it.
The partAdditional folder starts empty. This is where your additional part goes.

1. Copy accel_kernel_module.c and your program (part3.c or part4.c) from the part you
   started from into partAdditional. Then change them. Any program file name is fine,
   but keep the driver file named accel_kernel_module.c.

2. Copy accel_kernel_module.c even if you did not change it.
   ./runall.sh additional only loads the accel driver that is in this folder.

3. Keep only your changed version in this folder.
   Do not also put your original part code in here.

4. You do not need to write a Makefile. The one in the folder builds your files by itself.
   accel_kernel_module.c is built with ../ADXL345.c into the accel driver (/dev/accel).
   Your program is built with ../accel_wrappers.c, the same way parts 3 and 4 are.
   If you need new ADXL345 functions, add them to ../ADXL345.c. Do not change the ones
   your other parts use.

5. Header files go in the include folder, not in partAdditional.
   Include them the same way your other parts do:
       #include "../include/ADXL345.h"

6. Test it with ./runall.sh additional
   This loads your accel driver, then runs your program.

7. Fill in the ADDITIONAL PART section at the bottom of this README.
   I'll use it to find and test your work, so be specific.

To clean all directories and remove all drivers: ./runall clean


To submit your solution: ./runall.sh submit 
The script generates a .tar.bz2 archive. Please upload this
archive to quercus (only one team member needs to do this).


The script may generate errors, for example:
ERROR: Removing 'timer': No such file or directory
These errors can be ignored.


If you would like to clarify your interpretation of the exercise instructions 
or provide any additional information to the grader, PLEASE TYPE IT HERE:



ADDITIONAL PART (leave blank if you did not do one)

Which part it is based on (3 or 4):
Part 4.

What I added:
I added an ASCII tilt-controlled target game. The moving green bubble (o) is the player, and a red X is the target. The score increases by one whenever the bubble reaches the target, and a new target appears at another location. I kept the X, Y, and Z acceleration readings at the top of the terminal and used the Part 4 tap detection to reset the game.
Also, it is worth to mention that the bubble only needs to be within two columns and one row of the target to score. Targets are generated within a reasonably reachable range.

How to use it (what to do with the board, what shows on the terminal):
Run ./runall.sh additional. Keep the DE1-SoC board flat at first, then tilt it in the X and Y directions to move the green o toward the red X. When the bubble reaches the target, the score increases and a new X appears. The top of the terminal shows the acceleration values, current score, and instructions. Single-tap the board to reset the score to zero and generate a new target. Press Ctrl+C to quit.

